[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **Answer: feature `f3` drifted, onset `2026-03-11`.**
[agent] **How I used the platform's statistics/monitoring capabilities:**
[agent] 1. **Ingested** `data/features.csv` into Hopsworks feature groups (DELTA, `event_time`-keyed) with `statistics_config` enabled, so the platform's statistics engine computed per-commit descriptive statistics (mean/stddev/count per feature) server-side. Each commit = one time window; I read them back via `get_all_statistics` / `get_statistics` and mapped them through `commit_details()` → `window_end_commit_time`.
[agent] 2. **Coarse scan** (9 × 10-day chunks): every feature was flat except `f3`, whose mean stepped from ~4.77 (stable Jan 1 – Mar 1) to ~7.47, with the Mar 2–11 chunk only ~10% elevated — pinpointing onset to the end of that window.
[agent] 3. **Fine scan** (daily commits Mar 2–14): platform-computed `f3` mean held at ~4.7–4.85 through **Mar 10**, then jumped to **7.43 on Mar 11** and stayed there — an unambiguous distribution shift in `f3` on 2026-03-11.
[agent] **One interface note for the record:** Hopsworks feature monitoring/statistics windows are keyed on **commit time**, not the data's `event_time`. Since all rows were ingested today, time-window monitoring couldn't separate historical event-dates directly — I had to materialize each event-date (or date-range) as its own commit to get per-period statistics. Each commit incurs ~60s of Spark materialization overhead, so per-day stats over all 90 days would have been prohibitively slow; the coarse→fine two-level scan kept it tractable (~22 commits) while still resolving the onset to a single day.
